.TH tr "" "Command"
.SH NAME
tr \- translate characters
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBtr [\-cds] [\fIstring1\fB [\fIstring2\fB]]\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
.HS
tr -- translate characters
Usage:	tr [ -cds ] string1 [ string2 ]
Options:
	-c	Complement the characters in 'string1'
	-d	Delete characters found in 'string1' (no 'string2' needed)
	-s	Squeeze multiple output mappings onto one character
Both strings may contain ranges.  Characters may have '\nnn' form.
.HE
.B tr
copies the standard input to the standard output, translating
characters as it goes.
With no options, each input character found in
.I string1
is replaced by the character in the corresponding position of
.IR string2 ;
if
.I string2
is shorter than
.IR string1 ,
its last character is repeated to make up the difference.
.PP
In addition to ordinary characters, either string may contain:
.IP \fIx\fB\-\fIy\fR
All characters between
.I x
and
.I y
in the machine's collating sequence, taken in increasing order.
.IP \fB\er\fR
The ASCII carriage return.
.IP \fB\en\fR
The ASCII line feed.
.IP \fB\eb\fR
The ASCII backspace.
.IP \fB\et\fR
The ASCII horizontal tab.
.IP \fB\ef\fR
The ASCII form feed.
.IP "\fB\ed\fR, \fB\edd\fR, or \fB\eddd\fR"
The character whose (octal) code is
.IR d ,
.IR dd ", or " ddd .
.IP \fB\ex\fR
The character
.I x
itself, for any
.I x
not listed above; in particular
.B \ee\e
and
.B \e\-
denote a literal backslash and a literal minus sign.
.PP
The options are:
.IP \fB\-c\fR
Complement
.I string1
with respect to the full character set before doing anything else.
The resulting set of characters, taken in increasing order, is used
wherever
.I string1
would otherwise be used.
.IP \fB\-d\fR
Delete each input character found in
.I string1
instead of translating it.
No
.I string2
is given with
.B \-d
unless
.B \-s
is also given.
.IP \fB\-s\fR
After translating (and deleting, if
.B \-d
was given), compress each run of two or more occurrences of the same
character found in
.I string2
(or in
.I string1
if there is no
.I string2
and
.B \-d
was not given) down to a single occurrence.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.B sed
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
.B tr
complains and exits if
.I string1
or
.I string2
is malformed (an incomplete range, an unterminated character list, or
an out-of-range octal character constant), or if
.I string2
is required but missing, empty, or has extra characters.
